    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A water-soluble globular protein binds a small molecule in a pocket on its surface. In the normal folded protein, one amino acid with a hydrophobic R group sits deep in the protein's core, far from the binding pocket. In a variant protein, that amino acid is replaced by one with an ionic (charged) R group. Which prediction best explains how this change could affect the protein's function?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The charged amino acid breaks the peptide bonds next to it, splitting the polypeptide into two fragments that can no longer bind the small molecule.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses R-group chemistry with the backbone. Peptide bonds link the carboxyl group of one amino acid to the amine of the next, regardless of which R group is present, so the chain stays intact and only the interactions that shape folding change. Fix: Ask whether the change involves the backbone bond or the R group before predicting broken peptide bonds."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The protein's function is unchanged, because the substituted amino acid is far from the binding pocket and the pocket's own amino acids are the same.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes only residues touching the target matter. The core's R-group interactions determine the overall tertiary shape, so a disrupted core can shift the whole fold, including a distant pocket. Fix: Trace the chain sequence to interactions to overall shape to function before deciding a distant change has no effect."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Only the alpha-helices change, because R groups form the hydrogen bonds of secondary structure; the overall 3D shape and binding stay the same.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misassigns which atoms build each level of structure. Alpha-helices and beta-sheets form from hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, while R-group interactions such as hydrophobic clustering mainly determine tertiary shape, which governs the binding pocket. Fix: Check whether an interaction involves backbone atoms or R groups to decide which level of structure it affects."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The charged R group disrupts the hydrophobic interactions that hold the core together, altering the tertiary shape, which can distort the binding pocket and reduce binding.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "A sequence change alters R-group interactions. Hydrophobic R groups cluster in the core away from water, so a charged R group there disrupts those interactions and changes the 3D fold. Because function depends on shape, a changed fold can reshape even a distant binding pocket, reducing binding."
     }
    ]
   },
