   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A protein consisting of a single polypeptide must bind a particular small molecule to perform its function. A variant replaces one charged amino acid with an amino acid having a nonpolar R group. Both versions have the same chain length and intact peptide bonds, but the variant binds the molecule less effectively. Which explanation best connects the amino acid replacement to the reduced function?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Because the chain length is unchanged, the protein’s folding must be unchanged; reduced binding is unrelated to its shape.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: equating chain length with protein shape. Proteins of equal length can have different amino acid sequences and R-group interactions, producing different shapes and functions. Fix: Compare R-group properties, not just amino acid counts, before predicting whether folding will change."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The replacement breaks peptide bonds, fragmenting the polypeptide and eliminating the surface needed for binding.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: confusing altered R-group interactions with a broken backbone. The stem states that peptide bonds remain intact, so fragmentation cannot explain the reduced binding. Fix: Check whether the evidence indicates broken peptide bonds or altered interactions within an intact chain."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The replacement can alter R-group interactions, changing the protein’s folding and binding surface so that it binds the molecule less effectively.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Changing an amino acid changes the primary structure and can alter interactions that stabilize the protein’s three-dimensional shape. A changed binding surface can reduce binding and therefore function, completing the sequence → interactions → folding/shape → function chain."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The replacement disrupts the hydrogen bonds between R groups that define alpha helices, changing the surface needed for binding.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: assigning secondary structure to R-group hydrogen bonds. Alpha helices are stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, whereas R-group interactions help determine tertiary structure. Fix: Identify whether the proposed interaction involves backbone atoms or R groups before naming the affected structural level."
     }
    ]
